{
  "version": 2,
  "title": "Toy Models of Superposition",
  "author": "N. Elhage et al.",
  "year": 2022,
  "text": "Neural networks often pack many unrelated concepts into a single neuron - a puzzling phenomenon known as 'polysemanticity' which makes interpretability much more challenging. This paper provides a toy model where polysemanticity can be fully understood, arising as a result of models storing additional sparse features in \"superposition.\" We demonstrate the existence of a phase change, a surprising connection to the geometry of uniform polytopes, and evidence of a link to adversarial examples. We also discuss potential implications for mechanistic interpretability.",
  "source": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2209.10652",
